<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Trencavel</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Trencavel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trencavel were a family of viscounts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> who in the eleventh and twelfth centuries held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albi">Albi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beziers">Béziers</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nîmes</a> and Agde, shifting allegiance between the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. Accused of protecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a>, Viscount <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-roger-trencavel">Raymond-Roger Trencavel</a> lost Béziers and Carcassonne to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> in 1209 and died in prison. His son's attempts to recover the lands had failed by 1247.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-trencavel.jpg" alt="House of Trencavel" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gauthier LANGLOIS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ArmoiriesTrencavel.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
